const QUESTIONS: readonly BankQuestion[] = [
  // Part I: Human Action and Exchange (chapters 1–3). Chapter 1: Fundamentals of Human Action
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'How does Rothbard define human action?', 'Purposeful behavior', ['Any movement of the body', 'Behavior driven by instinct', 'Choices made by groups'],
    'It differs from reflexes and the movements of inorganic matter: it is governed by a purpose, the actor’s end.', 'What separates it from a reflex?'),
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'Who, for Rothbard, can have ends and act to attain them?', 'Only individuals', ['Societies as wholes', 'States and nations', 'Social classes'],
    'Saying “governments act” is a metaphor. Groups have no existence apart from the actions of their individual members.', 'Can a “collective” want anything?'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'Why doesn’t a man who desires sunshine act on his desire?', 'He knows of no means to bring it about', ['Sunshine has no value to anyone', 'He lacks the money to buy any sunshine', 'His desire is purely irrational'],
    'Action requires an end and “technological ideas” about how to reach it. Without believed means, there is no action.', 'Wanting is not enough.'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard reject the distinction between “economic goods” and “free goods” like air?', 'Air is a general condition, not a means', ['Air is in fact scarce everywhere', 'Every good must have a money price on the market', 'Air is owned by the government'],
    'Goods are the objects of economizing action. Air, being superabundant, is a general condition of welfare and not the object of action.', 'What makes something a good at all?'),
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'What are producers’ goods also called, in the book’s terms?', 'Factors of production', ['Consumers’ goods of the first order', 'General conditions of welfare', 'Free goods, like air and sunlight'],
    'Consumers’ goods serve wants directly; producers’ goods are transformed into them later. Rothbard traces Jones’s ham sandwich back through its stages.', 'Higher order than the sandwich itself.'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'Why must every consumers’ good require more than one scarce factor of production?', 'With only one, it would need no production', ['Laws require several suppliers', 'Single factors are always far too costly to use', 'Technology demands many machines'],
    'If the sandwich needed no carrying, time or land, it would already be in the armchair: the impossible assumption of Paradise.', 'Would the sandwich move itself?'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'What is “maturing time” in the period of production?', 'Time needed without concurrent labor', ['Time a worker spends in training', 'The years before a loan is finally repaid', 'The time a firm takes to grow up'],
    'Crops ripen and wine ages by the processes of nature, while working time is when labor is spent.', 'Think of wine aging in a cellar.'),
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'How does Rothbard resolve the “value paradox” of bread versus platinum?', 'People value specific units, not whole classes', ['Bread really is worth more than platinum, by law', 'Platinum is valued for the labor in it', 'Prices are set by government decree'],
    'Acting man asks whether a loaf of bread is worth more than an ounce of platinum, given the stocks available, not “bread in general.”', 'A loaf, or all the bread in the world?'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'A man with two cows and three horses saves the cows from a fire, yet would give up a cow before a horse. Why?', 'Units and whole stocks are valued differently', ['He is acting irrationally', 'Cows are worth more than horses', 'His preferences changed overnight during the fire'],
    'Choosing between one cow and one horse, he keeps the horse; choosing between whole stocks, he saves the cows.', 'One cow, or all the cows?'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'What is “marginal utility” in Rothbard’s sense?', 'The least important end the stock serves', ['The total value of the whole supply he owns', 'The average satisfaction per unit', 'The price paid for the last unit'],
    'Losing one horse, the owner gives up the lowest-ranked end, shifting Seabiscuit from pleasure riding to the wagon if need be.', 'What gets given up if one unit is lost?'),
  q(1, 1, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard reject grounding the law of marginal utility on a “law of satiation” of wants?', 'It rests on praxeology, not psychology', ['Satiation never happens in practice', 'Utility can be measured by machines', 'People always want more and more of everything'],
    'Psychological satiation is irrelevant to economics. Those writers wrongly concluded marginal utility might rise at first.', 'Ice cream scoops are a matter for psychologists.'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'Why did the mandrake weed lose all its value?', 'It was specific to a single, lost use', ['Its supply became unlimited all at once', 'The government banned it', 'Its labor cost fell to zero'],
    'When people stopped believing its extracts healed, the weed could not be converted to any other use.', 'It was good for only one thing.'),
  q(1, 1, 'hard', 'Robinson Crusoe, a skilled engineer, lands on an island without equipment. What does Rothbard show with him?', 'Knowledge alone can’t raise output', ['Technical skill is all that production needs', 'Engineers make the worst castaways', 'Islands always lack natural resources'],
    'A recipe sets outer limits on production, but actual increases need more productive factors.', 'He knows how to build a mansion. Can he?'),
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'For Rothbard, what is the only way to raise output per unit of time, given nature and limited labor?', 'Accumulate more capital goods', ['Work longer and longer hours forever', 'Print more money', 'Consume more today'],
    'Labor is limited by population and its disutility, so man must save and invest in capital goods.', 'Crusoe’s stick.'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'Crusoe picks 20 berries an hour. What must he give up to make a stick that lets him gather 50?', 'Ten hours of berries or leisure', ['Nothing, since sticks are free', 'Part of his island to a neighbor', 'All his future berry harvests'],
    'He must forgo about 200 berries or some leisure: saving, a sacrifice of present for greater future consumption.', 'Making the stick takes time.'),
  q(1, 1, 'medium', 'What is Crusoe doing if he doesn’t replace his worn-out stick and enjoys more berries now?', 'Consuming his capital', ['Saving for the future', 'Investing in new tools', 'Maintaining his capital'],
    'His time preference leads him to prefer more present consumption to the larger future loss when output falls back.', 'The stick won’t replace itself.'),
  q(1, 1, 'hard', 'Which economists, Rothbard says, wrongly assumed capital reproduces itself without new acts of saving?', 'J.B. Clark and Frank Knight', ['Ludwig von Mises and Böhm-Bawerk', 'Menger and Wieser', 'Hume and Cantillon'],
    'Replacing worn-out capital requires an independent act of saving, just as forming it did.', 'Two American economists.'),
  q(1, 1, 'easy', 'Why is even “doing nothing” a form of action, for Rothbard?', 'It is a choice among alternatives', ['It burns calories in the body', 'The law requires every citizen to act', 'Idleness produces leisure goods for sale'],
    'Man can always try to improve his lot, so spending time in leisure is itself a choice affecting his supply of goods.', 'Can you avoid choosing?'),
  q(1, 1, 'hard', 'What form does economic analysis take, according to Appendix A?', 'Logical deduction from the action axiom', ['Statistical testing of hypotheses', 'Laboratory experiments on real consumers', 'Historical case studies of nations'],
    'Assert A, the action axiom; if A then B, if B then C; therefore B, C, D. Economics says nothing about the content of ends.', 'Assert A, then follow the logic.'),

  // Chapter 2: Direct Exchange
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'What does Rothbard call the relationship in which a master forces a subject into exchange?', 'Hegemonic exchange, or exploitation', ['Autistic exchange, or self-help', 'Contractual exchange, or trade', 'Co-operation, or mutual aid'],
    'Only the ruler benefits, since only he chooses freely. Rothbard reserves “co-operation” for voluntary choices.', 'The opposite of contractual.'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'What is “autistic exchange”?', 'Exchange with no one else involved', ['Exchange between two strangers', 'Exchange forced by violence', 'Exchange using money as its medium'],
    'All of the isolated Crusoe’s exchanges, giving up one state of affairs for another, were autistic.', 'Crusoe alone on his island.'),
  q(1, 2, 'easy', 'Through praxeology, what do we know about every voluntary exchange?', 'Both parties expect to gain', ['One side always loses', 'The values traded are equal', 'The richer party gains more'],
    'Each values what he gets more than what he gives; otherwise he wouldn’t trade.', 'Why would anyone trade otherwise?'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'Crusoe trades 5,000 berries for Jackson’s cow. Is there an “equality of value” between them?', 'No: a double inequality of values', ['Yes: the price measures it', 'Yes: equal labor went into each of them', 'No: one of them was cheated'],
    'Crusoe values the cow more than the berries, Jackson less. Value lives in valuing minds, so there is nothing equal to measure.', 'Why does each one bother to trade?'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'Rothbard says feelings of friendship and community are more likely what, with respect to exchange?', 'The effect of co-operation, not its cause', ['The original cause of all exchange in history', 'Irrelevant to exchange entirely', 'Destroyed by the division of labor'],
    'Without the productive division of labor, each man’s gain would be another’s loss, and the world would be marked by perpetual war.', 'Which comes first?'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'What does Rothbard say about critics who accuse Adam Smith of assuming an “invisible hand” directs the market?', 'It’s a conclusion, not an assumption', ['Smith really did assume divine guidance in markets', 'The invisible hand is the State', 'The phrase was coined by Marx'],
    'The market benefits each participant whatever their motives. The “invisible hand” was simply a metaphor for that result.', 'Premise, or result?'),
  q(1, 2, 'easy', 'What does specialization, or the division of labor, depend on most?', 'The extent of the market', ['The size of the government', 'The number of factories', 'The supply of gold coins'],
    'The extent of the market sets the exchange-value a producer can obtain, and so how far he can specialize.', 'More buyers, more specialists.'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard say the concept of “elasticity of supply” is uninteresting?', 'Supply always rises with price', ['Supply can never be measured by anyone', 'Suppliers set prices by fiat', 'It applies only to money'],
    'Multiplying quantity supplied by price always rises with price. Elasticity of demand is meaningful; supply is always “elastic.”', 'Quantity supplied moves with price.'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard reject the calculus and “elasticity at a point” in economics?', 'Action deals in discrete steps', ['Economists are poor mathematicians', 'Calculus was invented too recently', 'Prices are never truly continuous'],
    'Utility can’t be measured, and differences too small to notice are treated as the same good. Continuous utility curves are “pernicious.”', 'Infinitely small differences don’t exist for action.'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'What is the “reservation demand” of sellers?', 'Their demand to keep their own stock', ['Their demand for a reserved seat at the auction', 'The minimum price set by law', 'Their demand for bank reserves'],
    'Added to buyers’ exchange demand, it makes up the “total demand to hold” the existing stock.', 'What sellers keep for themselves.'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'When a market reaches equilibrium and exchanges end, where have the goods gone?', 'To their most capable possessors', ['Back to their original owners in the end', 'To the government for storage', 'To those with the most money'],
    'Each good ends up with those who rank it highest on their value scales, relative to the other good.', 'Who values them most?'),
  q(1, 2, 'easy', 'Does economics deal only with material goods, according to Rothbard?', 'No: services count just the same', ['Yes: only physical goods have value', 'Yes: services cannot be exchanged', 'No: it deals only with money'],
    'Tangible goods are valued for their services. Medical advice and musical performances obey the same laws of price.', 'Is a concert a good?'),
  q(1, 2, 'medium', 'How does an unused natural resource become someone’s property in Rothbard’s free society?', 'By first use, mixing labor with it', ['By planting a flag and claiming it', 'By a grant from the government', 'By whoever can see it first'],
    'Columbus can’t claim a continent “as far as his eye can see.” He must cultivate or use the land.', 'Homesteading.'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'Why would “negotiable instruments” have no place in Rothbard’s free society?', 'They let a buyer keep stolen goods', ['They are too hard to print', 'Banks would refuse them', 'They pay far too little interest to holders'],
    'If A steals from B and sells to C, negotiability makes C the owner. Property rights require that the buyer seek restitution from the thief.', 'What happens to the true owner?'),
  q(1, 2, 'hard', 'How does Rothbard treat “external diseconomies” such as factory smoke?', 'As failures to defend property rights', ['As failures of the free market', 'As costs everyone must accept', 'As good reasons for public ownership of industry'],
    'Pollution that damages persons and property is an invasive act, like vandalism. The blame lies with the enforcing agency, not private property.', 'Whose rights are being invaded?'),

  // Chapter 3: The Pattern of Indirect Exchange
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'What is indirect exchange?', 'Buying a good to trade it again', ['Trading through a middleman', 'Exchange forced on people by the state', 'Barter between neighbors'],
    'A buys butter not to eat but because the shoemaker wants butter: butter becomes a medium of exchange.', 'Eggs for butter, butter for shoes.'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Why could a house-builder not run a business under direct exchange?', 'He couldn’t pay workers in pieces of house', ['Houses are illegal to barter', 'Workers would refuse to build for anyone', 'Lumber would cost too much'],
    'He would have to sell the house for exactly the mix of goods each mason and carpenter wanted. Production beyond the primitive would be impossible.', 'A piece of a house for each mason?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Why is butter more marketable than a plow?', 'Butter keeps its value when divided', ['Butter is always more useful than a plow', 'Plows are illegal to trade', 'Butter is produced by the state'],
    'A plow cut into pieces is nearly worthless, so a plow owner trades it for butter and uses the butter to buy what he wants.', 'Try cutting a plow in half.'),
  q(1, 3, 'easy', 'What does money make possible, according to Rothbard?', 'Advanced specialization and production', ['An end to scarcity for all', 'Equal incomes for everyone in society', 'Production without saving'],
    'It removes the problems of coincidence of wants and indivisibility, and allows ever more remote stages of production.', 'What did barter make impossible?'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Texans use the “Houston” of 20 grains of gold, and Bostonians the “Adams” of 10. What does Rothbard conclude?', 'The names are economically insignificant', ['The two currencies will float freely', 'One currency will drive out the other', 'The state must fix the rate of exchange between them'],
    'Both are units of weight of gold, so one Houston will exchange for two Adamses. The names only complicate matters.', 'Both are just weights of gold.'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'How was iron money in central Africa shaped, as Rothbard mentions?', 'So it could be split into two hoes', ['As rings worn around the neck', 'As small round coins with holes in the middle', 'As spearheads for hunting'],
    'Iron was valued as hoes, and the money form was made divisible into two parts, easily shaped into hoes.', 'A farm tool.'),
  q(1, 3, 'medium', 'Rothbard says it is nonsense to blame money for people’s preference for buyable goods. Why?', 'Each person chooses his own value scale', ['Money has no effect on anything people do at all', 'Nonexchangeable goods are worthless', 'The State sets everyone’s values'],
    'Some goods can’t be bought or sold, and how highly they rank is each person’s voluntary choice. Nothing in money compels it.', 'Who ranks the goods?'),
  q(1, 3, 'hard', 'What does Rothbard say about the common idea that only those in the fields or on assembly lines are “productive laborers”?', 'It is fallacious: managers labor too', ['It is correct: only manual work produces', 'It is true only under socialism', 'It applies only to farming nations'],
    'Laborers are all who expend labor in production, from ditchdigger to company president, including the self-employed investor.', 'Is a company president a laborer?'),

  // Part II: Prices, Production and Interest (chapters 4–6). Chapter 4: Prices and Consumption
  q(2, 4, 'easy', 'Compared with barter, what does money do to the number of markets needed?', 'Immeasurably reduces it', ['Doubles it at a stroke', 'Leaves it exactly the same as before', 'Multiplies it by the number of goods'],
    'Instead of a market of every good against every other, nearly every market has money as one of its two sides.', 'Every market now includes one common good.'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard reject treating money’s purchasing power as a measurable “price level”?', 'It is an array of particular prices', ['Prices never change at all', 'Only gold prices really matter', 'Statisticians are always biased in favor of the state'],
    'The classical economists meant the whole array of prices in all its particularity, not a statistical average of “all goods combined.”', 'Can you average horses, fish and eggs?'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'Who are the “submarginal” buyers and sellers on a market?', 'Those whose value scales exclude them', ['Those who trade only below cost', 'Those forbidden by law to trade', 'Those who arrive after the market closes'],
    'At the equilibrium price, the most capable buyers and sellers trade; the less eager are kept out by their own valuations.', 'Too unwilling at the going price.'),
  q(2, 4, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard reject measuring “consumers’ surplus”?', 'Psychic gains can’t be measured or compared', ['There is no such thing as a psychic gain', 'Consumers never gain anything at all from buying goods', 'Only producers ever earn a surplus'],
    'The supramarginal buyer gains, but his surplus can’t be contrasted with anyone else’s. Marshallians conveniently ignore goods that loom large.', 'Can one man’s satisfaction be added to another’s?'),
  q(2, 4, 'hard', 'What circularity does Mises’s “regression theorem” solve?', 'Money’s value rests on prior prices', ['Interest depends on the general rate of business profit', 'Wages depend on the prices of goods', 'Taxes depend on government spending'],
    'Today’s demand for money rests on yesterday’s prices, and so back to the day the commodity was valued only for its direct use.', 'Prices need money’s value; money’s value needs prices.'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'What is the difference between ex ante and ex post, for Rothbard?', 'Deciding versus judging results', ['Before taxes versus after taxes', 'Profits versus wages and rent', 'A planned economy versus a free market economy'],
    'Ex ante, an actor always expects a psychic profit. Ex post, he may find he lost.', 'Latin for “from before” and “from after.”'),
  q(2, 4, 'easy', 'Is the free market “unplanned,” according to Rothbard?', 'No: every individual plans for himself', ['Yes: no one plans anything on it', 'Yes: that is exactly why it needs the state to plan', 'No: big firms plan it for everyone'],
    'From a steel plant to a boy spending two cents on candy, all action is planned. Exchange co-ordinates the plans.', 'Who makes the plans?'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'What is the law of consumer action in a market economy?', 'Buy until utility stops beating price', ['Spend the same amount on every good', 'Buy the cheapest goods first', 'Spend all income as soon as it is received'],
    'He buys units until the marginal utility of adding one ceases to be greater than the utility of the money it costs.', 'When does he stop buying suits?'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'Why is a TV set lasting ten years worth more than an identical one lasting five?', 'It holds more future services', ['It costs more to manufacture in the factory', 'It is heavier and more solid', 'The law sets its price higher'],
    'Durable goods are valued for the stream of services they will provide, discounted for time preference.', 'What does a consumer really buy?'),
  q(2, 4, 'hard', 'Can we say a dollar means less to Rockefeller than to a poor man?', 'No: utilities can’t be compared', ['Yes: it’s a law of economics', 'Yes: the poor man needs it more', 'No: because money has no utility for rich men at all'],
    'Each man’s dollar would be worth more if he grew poorer. But Rockefeller may enjoy a dollar more than an ascetic poor man does.', 'Can you compare two minds?'),
  q(2, 4, 'hard', 'Does a consumer “equalize” marginal utility with the price of a good, as textbooks say?', 'No: the two always differ in rank', ['Yes: in equilibrium they are equal', 'Yes: but only for divisible goods', 'No: price is always higher'],
    'Jones buys two suits because the second still ranks above its price. Even with butter by the ounce, there is a difference in rank, never equality.', 'Utilities are ranked, not measured.'),
  q(2, 4, 'hard', 'Jones picks A over B about half the time. What does that show, for Rothbard?', 'His preferences shifted, not indifference', ['He is indifferent between A and B', 'He is choosing completely at random each time', 'He cannot make up his mind at all'],
    'Praxeology studies value scales revealed through choice. Each choice reveals a preference; the scale changed between choices.', 'Each time, he chose one.'),
  q(2, 4, 'medium', 'Rothbard distinguishes subjective valuation from “appraisement.” What is appraisement?', 'Estimating a good’s future money price', ['Ranking goods on one’s own value scale', 'Fixing prices by government decree', 'Measuring utility in units of gold'],
    'Jones appraising his house at 250 ounces isn’t ranking it on his scale. Appraisement underlies all entrepreneurial investment.', 'How much could it be sold for?'),

  // Chapter 5: Production: The Structure
  q(2, 5, 'easy', 'What is the law of time preference?', 'An end is preferred sooner rather than later', ['Goods lose their value as they age', 'Interest rates always rise over time', 'Future needs always outweigh present ones'],
    'Every person prefers satisfying an end in the present to satisfying the same end in the future.', 'Now or later?'),
  q(2, 5, 'medium', 'What is the “evenly rotating economy” (ERE)?', 'An imagined economy without change', ['The real economy at its peak', 'A model of perfect central planning', 'An economy run by a council whose members rotate'],
    'It can never exist, nor be consistently conceived, since no one would hold money. But it shows the direction of actual change.', 'Change has stopped.'),
  q(2, 5, 'hard', 'Why couldn’t money exist in the evenly rotating economy?', 'With certainty, no one holds cash', ['Banks would be abolished there by general agreement', 'Gold would be used up entirely', 'The state would forbid money'],
    'Everyone would know exactly what they’d spend and when, so nobody would keep a cash balance, and the monetary market would break down.', 'Why hold money if you know the future?'),
  q(2, 5, 'medium', 'Why can’t factors be compared as “equally suitable” in purely technological terms?', 'Different products have no common unit', ['Machines are always unique', 'Engineers disagree on how to measure machine output', 'Technology changes every year'],
    'Three units of A and two of B have no common physical measure. Only consumers’ valuations rank them.', 'Three of one, two of another.'),
  q(2, 5, 'medium', 'Is a man who is “free to starve” a condemnation of the free market?', 'No: poverty is a fact of nature', ['Yes: freedom causes poverty', 'Yes: only the state feeds people', 'No: nobody starves in markets'],
    'Crusoe is absolutely free and near starvation. Every child arrives without capital; the free market is the instrument to reduce poverty.', 'Crusoe is free.'),
  q(2, 5, 'easy', 'What is “cost,” in the subjective sense Rothbard uses?', 'The next best utility forgone', ['The money paid for inputs', 'The labor time spent producing', 'The price set by accountants'],
    'Costs are subjective and can’t be precisely determined by outside observers or ex post by accountants.', 'What did you give up?'),
  q(2, 5, 'medium', 'What is the essential contribution of capitalists to production?', 'Advancing money before the sale', ['Owning land that others work', 'Directing workers on the shop floor every day', 'Inventing new technologies'],
    'They pay factor owners while the product is being made, sparing them the wait. For this they earn interest.', 'Who pays the workers before the product sells?'),
  q(2, 5, 'medium', 'Why can labor only be hired, never bought, on the free market?', 'A free man’s will is inalienable', ['Workers are too costly to buy', 'Unions forbid outright purchases', 'Labor has no future value'],
    'Buying a man’s total future services would be slavery, and even “voluntary slavery” can’t be enforced on the free market.', 'Selling oneself.'),
  q(2, 5, 'hard', 'Rothbard calls the uniform discount on future goods throughout the economy what?', 'The pure rate of interest', ['The natural rate of profit', 'The marginal efficiency of capital', 'The rate of exploitation'],
    'It is the agio, the premium of present goods over future goods: the “price of time” set by all individual time preferences.', 'The price of time.'),
  q(2, 5, 'hard', 'In the evenly rotating economy, what happens to entrepreneurial profits and losses?', 'They disappear entirely', ['They become equal for all firms', 'They grow without limit', 'They turn into rents for landowners'],
    'With certainty, money costs including interest equal total sales for every firm. Only interest remains.', 'No uncertainty, no…?'),
  q(2, 5, 'hard', 'How are factor prices set if all factors are purely specific?', 'Only by bargaining, within wide zones', ['Exactly by their marginal physical products', 'By the labor time they contain', 'By a fixed rule set by the state'],
    'As in two-person barter, there are wide zones between the maximum price of one and the minimum of another.', 'Like a single buyer and seller.'),

  // Chapter 6: Production: The Rate of Interest and Its Determination
  q(2, 6, 'easy', 'What determines the pure rate of interest, according to Rothbard?', 'Individual time preferences', ['The central bank’s policy', 'The productivity of machines', 'The supply of loans by banks'],
    'The market for present against future goods, the “time market,” sets it, and it is grounded in time-preference schedules.', 'Present versus future.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Why is money “the present good par excellence”?', 'It is the one completely marketable good', ['It is the only good that never decays or wears out', 'Governments guarantee its value', 'It earns interest while held'],
    'It’s the open sesame to consumption goods whenever its owner wants. Future goods are present expectations of future money.', 'Exchangeable for anything at any time.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Why does every person’s time preference eventually become infinite as he saves more?', 'Everyone must consume today', ['Interest rates fall to zero', 'Banks refuse further deposits', 'Future money becomes worthless to him at that point'],
    'As present money dwindles its marginal utility rises, drastically limiting saving whatever the interest rate.', 'You still have to eat today.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'What does a sudden, massive rise in capitalists’ time preferences do?', 'Raises interest and cuts saving', ['Lowers interest and raises saving', 'Has no effect on the economy', 'Raises saving and output'],
    'Time preferences determine both the interest rate and the proportion of income saved and consumed. A huge rise would push society back toward barbarism.', 'Present satisfactions become worth much more.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Is there a separate class of “capitalists” set off from laborers and landowners?', 'No: anyone with money can be a capitalist', ['Yes: the wealthy alone can save', 'Yes: capitalists are born, not made, in every society', 'No: capitalists do not exist at all'],
    'Economics studies functions, not stratified classes. Whoever supplies present goods on the time market is a capitalist.', 'Functions, not persons.'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'Rothbard contrasts the thrifty French peasant with the rich playboy. What point does he make?', 'Time preference is each person’s own', ['The rich always save more', 'The poor never save anything', 'Peasants are better economists than rich playboys'],
    'That the rich generally save more may be a historical judgment, but it is no economic law.', 'Can a rich man run into debt?'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'Rothbard calls the importance of the producers’ loan market what?', 'A myth', ['The heart of capitalism', 'A Keynesian discovery', 'An empirical law'],
    'Interest exists in the production structure even with no producers’ loans at all. The loan market is dependent, not an independent force.', 'Is it really the heart of the matter?'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'How does the rate of interest tend to be equalized “in three dimensions”?', 'Across processes, stages and loans', ['Across banks, business firms and all levels of government', 'Across cities, states and nations', 'Across past, present and future'],
    'Horizontally in every process, vertically at every stage, and in depth in the consumer loan market, through arbitrage.', 'Horizontally, vertically and in depth.'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'How does Rothbard view the claim that corporate managers don’t aim to maximize stockholders’ income?', 'Long-run earnings are stockholder income', ['It is correct: managers ignore owners', 'It proves corporations must be regulated', 'It shows markets fail in big business'],
    'To “improve long-run earnings” is identical with maximizing stockholders’ income. The corporation doesn’t act apart from its owners.', 'What else builds managers’ prestige?'),
  q(2, 6, 'easy', 'If the general interest rate rises from 5 to 10 percent, what happens to a 5 percent bond priced at 100?', 'Its price falls to about 50', ['Its price rises to 200', 'Its price stays at 100', 'Its coupon is raised by law to 10'],
    'Long-term bonds trade on a flexible market. The yield on the market price, not the fixed coupon, adjusts to the general rate.', 'A fixed 5 must yield 10 percent.'),
  q(2, 6, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard reject Schumpeter’s claim that interest would be zero in the evenly rotating economy?', 'Positive time preference always exists', ['Schumpeter ignored the role of banks in creating credit', 'Interest is set by government decree', 'The ERE has no capital goods at all'],
    'Capital is maintained only because capitalists are paid interest to keep it up. At a zero rate, complete capital consumption would follow.', 'Does capital maintain itself?'),
  q(2, 6, 'medium', 'Why can investors earn a slightly lower return in a process they particularly enjoy?', 'They “consume” the enjoyment of investing', ['The state taxes popular industries', 'Popular firms are always less efficient than others', 'Enjoyment makes the product cheaper'],
    'A significant aggregate liking or dislike for a line of production adds a psychic component to its return.', 'Paying for a pleasure.'),

  // Part III: Factors, Entrepreneurs and Incomes (chapters 7–9). Chapter 7: Production: General Pricing of the Factors
  q(3, 7, 'easy', 'On the market, what does the price of a factor’s unit service tend to equal?', 'Its discounted marginal value product', ['The labor time needed to produce it', 'The cost of its own production, plus a markup', 'Whatever its owner thinks is fair'],
    'Rothbard abbreviates it DMVP: the factor’s marginal value product, discounted by the pure rate of interest.', 'Rothbard abbreviates it DMVP.'),
  q(3, 7, 'medium', 'Which factors can the market directly isolate and price by their marginal product?', 'The nonspecific factors', ['Only the purely specific ones', 'Only the factors used in fixed proportions', 'Only factors owned by the government'],
    'A specific factor is isolable only if it’s the only specific one in a combination: then it earns what’s left after paying the nonspecific ones.', 'Those usable in many lines.'),
  q(3, 7, 'medium', 'What happens to factor prices (DMVPs) when the rate of interest rises?', 'They fall', ['They rise', 'They stay the same', 'They rise for labor, fall for land'],
    'A higher rate of discount lowers every hypothetical discounted MVP. The rise itself comes from higher time preferences.', 'Discount the same product more steeply.'),
  q(3, 7, 'hard', 'In which region of its returns is every factor employed?', 'Diminishing marginal physical product', ['Increasing marginal physical product', 'Zero or negative marginal physical product', 'Negative marginal physical product'],
    'Each factor is used where its MPP, and still more its MVP, is falling. So adding units lowers its marginal product.', 'More of it, less per unit.'),
  q(3, 7, 'hard', 'At a 5 percent rate of interest, a factor has a DMVP of 6. On a ten-year process, what is its MVP?', 'About 10', ['Exactly 6', 'About 5', 'About 60'],
    'The more remote a stage is from consumption, the more the MVP must exceed the DMVP, to pay capitalists for advancing present goods that long.', 'The interest must be earned over ten years.'),
  q(3, 7, 'medium', 'Are wages paid out of capital or out of consumption, in Rothbard’s view?', 'Mostly out of saved capital', ['Out of consumption, as Henry George said', 'Out of taxes collected by the state', 'Out of bank credit created for them'],
    'Only factors in the very last stages, like doctors and artists, are paid directly by consumers. All others are paid in advance by saved capital.', 'Who pays workers before the product sells?'),
  q(3, 7, 'medium', 'How does Rothbard define “rent”?', 'The price of any good’s unit services', ['Income from land alone', 'Unearned income of any kind, from any source at all', 'A tax paid to a landlord'],
    'Following Frank Fetter, rent is hire: it applies to labor (wages), land, capital goods, and even nondurable goods.', 'Think of “renting out.”'),
  q(3, 7, 'hard', 'Is a depleting copper mine land or a capital good, in Rothbard’s classification?', 'Land: man can’t reproduce it', ['A capital good: it wears out with use', 'Neither: it is a consumers’ good', 'A capital good: men must dig it out'],
    'The test is whether labor and land must work to reproduce it. A mine is nonpermanent but can never be reproduced by man.', 'Can men produce another copper mine?'),
  q(3, 7, 'hard', 'Whose school, Rothbard says, attacks time preference and bases interest solely on capital’s “productivity”?', 'Frank Knight’s', ['Böhm-Bawerk’s', 'Frank Fetter’s', 'Ludwig von Mises’s'],
    'Rothbard answers Earl Rolph, a Knightian who rejected the discounted marginal productivity doctrine.', 'A Chicago economist.'),

  // Chapter 8: Production: Entrepreneurship and Change
  q(3, 8, 'easy', 'What new kind of income appears in a changing economy that doesn’t exist in the evenly rotating economy?', 'Entrepreneurial profit and loss', ['Wages for skilled labor', 'Interest on borrowed capital', 'Rent on land'],
    'In the ERE there is only interest and the prices of factor services. Uncertainty creates profits and losses.', 'Uncertainty is what makes it possible.'),
  q(3, 8, 'medium', 'What does the capitalist-entrepreneur look for, according to Rothbard?', 'Chances to beat the interest rate', ['Ways to avoid all risk completely', 'Government contracts with fixed, guaranteed prices', 'Workers willing to accept lower wages'],
    'If the rate is 5 percent and he thinks factors bought for 100 can yield 110, he invests. His arbitrage pushes the economy toward the ERE.', 'Buy for 100, sell for more than 105.'),
  q(3, 8, 'medium', 'How does net saving raise output even without any new technology?', 'It lengthens the structure of production', ['It raises prices and so profits', 'It cuts the number of workers needed in every industry', 'It lowers the cost of land'],
    'New investment goes into more and longer stages. Longer processes have higher physical productivity per original factor.', 'More stages.'),
  q(3, 8, 'hard', 'After net saving and investment, what happens over time to the profits that first appear?', 'They are imputed to factors and vanish', ['They grow larger every year after, without limit', 'They are taxed away by the state', 'They move into the banking system'],
    'In the new ERE, profits disappear, real wages and real rents rise, and the real value of ground land increases.', 'Where do profits end up?'),
  q(3, 8, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard, following Mises, dislike Böhm-Bawerk’s phrase “roundabout methods of production”?', 'Men seek the shortest way to ends', ['Böhm-Bawerk was wrong about capital', 'Production never takes any time', 'Roundabout methods are always wasteful of resources'],
    'Mises: it is better to speak of the higher physical productivity of processes requiring more time.', 'Who chooses the long way around?'),
  q(3, 8, 'hard', 'Why won’t a new investment be made in a shorter process than those already used?', 'Shorter productive ones came first', ['Short processes are banned by law', 'Long processes are always cheaper', 'Investors dislike quick returns'],
    'Given time preference, the shortest and most productive processes are adopted first. Further investment must go into longer ones.', 'Which would you have picked earlier?'),
  q(3, 8, 'medium', 'Why don’t firms immediately scrap old equipment when more productive techniques appear?', 'Bygones are bygones; old capital is still used', ['Laws forbid scrapping old machines', 'New techniques never really work', 'Workers refuse to learn new methods of production'],
    'Railroads overbuilt before trucks and planes still run on their existing equipment. Decisions rest on current and future costs.', 'The money is already spent.'),
  q(3, 8, 'hard', 'How does Rothbard treat Schumpeter’s emphasis on the entrepreneur as innovator?', 'Innovation is just one form of adjustment', ['It captures the whole of entrepreneurship', 'Innovation plays no role at all in markets', 'Only governments can truly innovate'],
    'Most entrepreneurs invest within a large shelf of known techniques. Output is limited by capital, not know-how.', 'Is every entrepreneur an inventor?'),
  q(3, 8, 'easy', 'For the economy to keep progressing, what must happen to the pure rate of interest?', 'It must keep falling', ['It must keep rising', 'It must stay fixed for good', 'It must reach zero'],
    'More investment requires lower time preferences, and so a falling pure rate. A constant rate means capital is only maintained.', 'Lower time preferences.'),
  q(3, 8, 'hard', 'Does a larger stock of capital lower the interest rate, as often claimed?', 'No: a lower rate increases capital', ['Yes: interest is the price of capital', 'Yes: abundant capital is cheap', 'No: capital has no effect on anything'],
    'The causation runs the other way: time preferences set the rate, and a lower rate raises the stock and value of capital goods.', 'Which causes which?'),
  q(3, 8, 'medium', 'Why are market interest rates higher in backward nations, at least partly?', 'A higher entrepreneurial risk component', ['Their people are less intelligent', 'Their governments ban or punish private saving', 'Their pure rate is set by law'],
    'Real rates include a component for uncertainty, so the comparison doesn’t show pure rates differ.', 'Not only time preference.'),
  q(3, 8, 'medium', 'What distinction between “risk” and “uncertainty” does Rothbard take from Frank Knight?', 'Risk is measurable over many like cases', ['Risk applies only to banks and insurers', 'Uncertainty can always be insured', 'Risk exists only under socialism'],
    'A bolt maker knows 1 percent are defective: that’s a cost. Fire is insurable across many firms. Entrepreneurial uncertainty is not.', 'Defective bolts versus the unknown future.'),

  // Chapter 9: Production: Particular Factor Prices and Productive Incomes
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'Which factors earn net rents, as opposed to gross rents?', 'Only labor and land', ['Only capital goods', 'Every kind of good', 'Only money balances'],
    'A capital good’s gross rents are imputed to the factors that produced it, so its owners earn only interest.', 'The original factors.'),
  q(3, 9, 'hard', 'Rothbard says costs don’t determine prices. What is the true relation?', 'Consumers’ prices determine factor prices', ['Labor costs set all final prices', 'Government sets both prices and costs by law', 'Prices and costs are unrelated'],
    'The cost-of-production theory of value is wrong. Prices of consumers’ goods determine, through the market, the prices of land and labor.', 'Which way does the causation run?'),
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'Rothbard notes that even union advocates find unionizing vice presidents absurd. What does he conclude?', 'Managers and workers are all labor', ['Vice presidents should form unions', 'Unions are needed only for managers', 'Only manual workers are laborers'],
    'There is no real labor-management dichotomy. If unionizing vice presidents is absurd, perhaps so is unionizing any workers.', 'Where does “labor” end?'),
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'Why does TV-directing pay more than ditch-digging, though people like it better?', 'Its skill is scarcer and more productive', ['Television companies are monopolies', 'Ditch-diggers lack strong unions', 'The market rewards the wrong things, by chance'],
    'A psychic component lowers the pay of liked jobs, but it is outweighed by the higher marginal value productivity of the rarer skill.', 'How many people can direct TV?'),
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'Why have wage rates risen in advancing economies, according to Rothbard?', 'More capital per worker', ['Workers became more energetic', 'Unions won higher pay', 'Laws set higher minimums'],
    'The causal agents are capitalist-entrepreneurs: workers have more and better tools, so labor becomes relatively scarcer.', 'Who provided the tools?'),
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'An employer pays for pension and “welfare” benefits. What happens to his overt wage rates?', 'They are correspondingly lower', ['They stay the same as rivals’', 'They rise to match the benefits', 'They are set by the government'],
    'Total pay can’t exceed the worker’s DMVP. The worker in effect chooses the form his pay takes.', 'He can’t pay more than the worker’s product.'),
  q(3, 9, 'easy', 'Can there be an involuntary “unemployment problem” on the free market, according to Rothbard?', 'No: any labor can be sold at a market wage', ['Yes: capitalism always creates unemployment', 'Yes: there are never enough jobs', 'No: everyone is forced to work'],
    'Whoever insists on a particular job, place or minimum wage may stay idle, but by his own volition.', 'What clears any market?'),
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'What is the real problem, for Rothbard, if not employment as such?', 'Employment at an above-subsistence wage', ['Finding jobs in the right industry for each person', 'Keeping everyone busy all day', 'Ending technological progress'],
    'Scarce land or little capital per worker can push some wages below subsistence; then the infirm must rely on gifts.', 'Nobody wants a job for nothing.'),
  q(3, 9, 'hard', 'Where does an innovation shift workers from and to?', 'From inelastic to elastic demand industries', ['From cities to the countryside', 'From private firms to the state', 'From skilled jobs to unskilled, routine jobs'],
    'Cheaper goods with inelastic demand mean less spending there; with elastic demand, more. New machine-making industries also hire.', 'Where does the extra spending go?'),
  q(3, 9, 'hard', 'Why would a world of producers’ co-operatives break down, according to Rothbard?', 'There would be no way to calculate', ['Workers would refuse to co-operate', 'The state would tax them too much', 'They would produce far too much'],
    'Without separate markets for labor, land and capital, there are no prices to allocate factors rationally.', 'What would they lack, if not prices?'),
  q(3, 9, 'hard', 'What limits how far a firm can integrate vertically, for Rothbard?', 'It needs outside markets to calculate', ['Antitrust laws set a maximum size', 'Managers can only supervise so many people at once', 'Technology caps every firm’s output'],
    'A firm swallowing every stage would have no market price for its intermediate goods. That “bit of chaos” brings losses.', 'Calculation needs market prices.'),
  q(3, 9, 'medium', 'Why would “international trade” not be a special problem on a purely free market?', 'Nations would not be economic units', ['No trade would cross borders', 'All nations would end up producing the same goods', 'Trade would be run by governments'],
    'With no barriers or currency differences, it is just interspatial trade. Interest, prices and wages tend to be uniform worldwide.', 'Would borders matter?'),
  q(3, 9, 'hard', 'Formerly predatory wealth enters a free market. Does it bias the market for good, in Rothbard’s view?', 'No: losses shift it to able producers', ['Yes: forever, through inheritance', 'Yes: until the state confiscates and redistributes it', 'No: because it is always spent fast'],
    'To keep ill-gotten gains, former robbers must invest to serve consumers, and their predation hardly trained them for that.', 'Can robbers manage capital well?'),

  // Part IV: Monopoly and Money (chapters 10–11). Chapter 10: Monopoly and Competition
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'What term does Rothbard prefer to “consumers’ sovereignty”?', 'Individual self-sovereignty', ['Producers’ sovereignty over markets', 'Popular sovereignty in the economy', 'Sovereignty of the price system'],
    'A producer’s dependence on consumers is voluntary and revocable. Each person is sovereign over his own person and property.', 'Who is really sovereign over what?'),
  q(4, 10, 'hard', 'Which economist originated the concept of “consumers’ sovereignty,” which Rothbard criticizes?', 'W.H. Hutt', ['Edward Chamberlin', 'Joan Robinson', 'Alfred Marshall'],
    'Hutt, of the University of Cape Town, used it as an ideal against which to measure the free market.', 'A professor at the University of Cape Town.'),
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'On the free market, what is the only criterion of a “just” price, according to Rothbard?', 'The free-market price', ['The competitive price', 'The cost of production', 'A price set by the courts'],
    'The alternative to the free-market price is hegemonic price-fixing: outlawing free exchanges and exploiting man by man.', 'Whatever free exchanges set.'),
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'What essential difference does Rothbard see between a cartel and an ordinary corporation?', 'None: both pool assets under one management', ['Cartels are always illegal conspiracies', 'Corporations never set prices themselves', 'Cartels cover more than one product line'],
    '“Collusion” and “conspiracy” are emotive words. Pooling assets under agreed rules is the same in both.', 'What happens when a partnership forms?'),
  q(4, 10, 'easy', 'Who can determine the most efficient size of a firm, in Rothbard’s view?', 'Only entrepreneurs themselves', ['Engineers studying the technology', 'Economists using cost curves', 'Antitrust regulators in Washington'],
    'Economics can’t tell us optimum size. Consumers’ wishes are “telegraphed” through prices, and profit-seeking does the rest.', 'Not economists, not engineers.'),
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard call the cartel an inherently unstable form?', 'Members either merge or break away', ['Courts always dissolve them in time', 'Consumers boycott every cartel', 'They can only last one season'],
    'If joint action pays, they merge into one firm. If not, some members break away, quotas cause bickering, and newcomers expand.', 'Profitable, or not?'),
  q(4, 10, 'hard', 'Hutt said consumers are free only to the extent the “power of substitution” exists. What is Rothbard’s reply?', 'It confuses freedom with abundance', ['Consumers have no freedom at all on markets', 'Substitution is always unlimited anyway', 'Only the State can create substitutes'],
    'Crusoe and Friday have little choice, yet are absolutely free if neither invades the other. Critics may enter the industry themselves.', 'Is Crusoe unfree on his island?'),
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'Why could “One Big Cartel” of all firms never be established on the free market?', 'It couldn’t calculate without markets', ['Antitrust laws would forbid it at once', 'Workers would refuse to work for it', 'Consumers would stop buying altogether'],
    'With no external markets for its factors, it couldn’t allocate rationally and would suffer severe losses. Socialism is One Big Cartel imposed by the State.', 'The limits of calculability.'),
  q(4, 10, 'hard', 'What was Lord Coke’s classic, original definition of monopoly?', 'An exclusive grant by the king', ['A single seller of a unique product', 'A firm with control over its price', 'A firm making above-normal profits for years'],
    'This is the definition that gave “monopoly” its sinister connotations. On the free market such a privilege can’t exist.', 'It came from the Crown.'),
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'Why can’t all prices be monopoly prices, even granting monopoly-price theory?', 'Consumers can’t spend more on everything', ['Governments cap prices in every industry', 'Producers always prefer to sell more', 'Gold supplies are too small to allow it'],
    'With a given money stock, more spent on one good means less on others. At most about half the industries could have monopoly prices.', 'A fixed total of spending.'),
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'A firm cuts prices and drives a weaker rival out. What does Rothbard see wrong with “cutthroat competition”?', 'Nothing: the better firm serves consumers', ['It always creates a harmful monopoly', 'It should be outlawed by antitrust law', 'It hurts workers more than any tariff'],
    'Losses show a firm isn’t serving consumers as well; factors shift to the efficient firm. Only the miscalculating entrepreneur is hurt.', 'Who lost, and why?'),
  q(4, 10, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard say there is no such thing as “selling below costs” on stock already produced?', 'Once produced, the sale is costless', ['Costs always exceed prices', 'Accountants can always find the true costs', 'The law forbids it anyway'],
    'Costs are opportunities forgone along the path of decisions to produce. Once the stock exists, nothing is forgone by selling it.', 'What is given up by selling?'),
  q(4, 10, 'easy', 'What does Rothbard conclude about the concept of “monopoly price” on the free market?', 'It is meaningless: only market prices exist', ['It is common and should be regulated', 'It applies only to large corporations, not small firms', 'It exists only in the short run'],
    'No criterion separates a “monopoly price” from a “competitive price.” There is only the free-market price.', 'Can anyone tell them apart?'),
  q(4, 10, 'hard', 'Can a cement firm in Rochester earn a “locational monopoly price,” in Rothbard’s view?', 'No: sites differ as goods', ['Yes: distance always creates monopolies', 'Yes: until a railroad reaches Rochester', 'No: cement prices are set by the state'],
    'Prices at different locations can’t be compared, and no site can be split into units to be sold and withheld. Idle sites are submarginal.', 'Is cement in Rochester the same good as cement in Albany?'),
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'What happens when a union imposes a wage above the market rate in a growing industry?', 'It blocks expansion, destroying potential jobs', ['It raises all workers’ wages for good, everywhere', 'It creates more jobs in that industry', 'It has no effect on employment at all'],
    'It may not fire current workers, but prevents the industry from expanding in response to demand. Open unions also draw more workers into idleness.', 'Jobs in the making.'),
  q(4, 10, 'hard', 'How does Rothbard answer the union argument that wages are “indeterminate” within a wide zone?', 'The zone is very small in modern markets', ['Unions can push wages anywhere within it safely', 'Wages are fixed precisely by law', 'Zones exist only for managers'],
    'Wide zones belong to two-person barter. With many buyers and sellers, the room for “bargaining” is tiny.', 'Barter versus big markets.'),
  q(4, 10, 'hard', 'Rothbard asks if Crusoe and Friday trading fish for lumber are “bilateral monopolists.” What point is he making?', 'Numbers prove nothing', ['Two traders always exploit each other', 'Island trade should be regulated', 'Monopoly is natural on small islands'],
    'If the State can’t justly arrest Crusoe and Friday, how can it coerce markets with far more competitors?', 'How many traders are “enough”?'),
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'What “ideal” does the theory of monopolistic or imperfect competition uphold, according to Rothbard?', 'Pure competition, with perfectly elastic demand', ['Free competition among any number of firms', 'A single efficient firm in each industry', 'Government-run firms in key industries'],
    'Chamberlin and Joan Robinson measured the real world against a fallacious ideal in which no firm can affect its price.', 'A perfectly horizontal demand curve.'),
  q(4, 10, 'medium', 'Why can persistent multiform prices for a resalable good not last on the free market?', 'Arbitrageurs buy cheap and resell at market', ['The law forbids charging two prices', 'Sellers always know the market price', 'Consumers refuse to pay different prices'],
    'An ignorant seller underpricing steel will be bought out by resellers, outbid by other buyers, and soon informed or out of business.', 'Someone will buy low and sell high.'),
  q(4, 10, 'easy', 'Which of patents and copyrights does Rothbard consider consistent with the free market?', 'Copyrights, but not patents', ['Patents, but not copyrights', 'Both patents and copyrights', 'Neither patents nor copyrights'],
    'A copyright rests on a contract against reproduction. A patent bars even an independent inventor from using his own invention.', 'Which one stops an independent inventor?'),
  q(4, 10, 'hard', 'How could an inventor protect his machine on the free market without a patent?', 'Sell it under a “copyright”', ['Keep it locked in a vault forever', 'Ask the government for a subsidy', 'Sell it only to his own relatives'],
    'Buyers would agree not to reproduce it for sale; violating that would be implicit theft. Independent inventors would remain free.', 'Extend the copyright system.'),
  q(4, 10, 'hard', 'What is Rothbard’s objection to the claim that patents encourage “sufficient” research?', 'No standard of “too little” exists', ['Research is always wasteful anyway', 'Patents produce far too much useless research', 'Only the State should do research'],
    'Resources have countless alternative uses. The market’s standard is service to consumers; no outside observer can say research is “insufficient.”', 'Too much, too little: by what standard?'),

  // Chapter 11: Money and Its Purchasing Power
  q(4, 11, 'easy', 'What two factors determine the purchasing power of money (PPM)?', 'The demand to hold money and its stock', ['The government and the policies of the central bank', 'Interest rates and the trade balance', 'Wages and the productivity of labor'],
    'If people want to hold more money than exists at the current PPM, they bid it up, and prices fall, until the two are equal.', 'Supply and demand, for money.'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'What does Rothbard say of classifying monetary theories into “quantity,” “cash balance” and “commodity” theories?', 'All the elements belong together', ['The quantity theory alone is correct', 'Only the commodity theory is valid', 'They are irreconcilable rival schools'],
    'Money is a commodity, its quantity matters, the demand for cash balances matters, and the analysis applies to incomes and spending.', 'Pick one, or all of them?'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'Why must money’s subjective utility be analyzed further than that of other goods?', 'It depends on money’s objective exchange-value', ['Money has no utility at all for anyone', 'Its utility is fixed by legal tender laws and courts', 'Money is valued only for its metal content'],
    'Apart from its nonmonetary uses, money is useful only as a medium of exchange, so its utility depends on what it can buy.', 'You value it for what it buys.'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'Even “idle” cash in a balance serves a purpose in the real world. What is it?', 'Readiness for an uncertain future', ['Earning interest from the bank on deposits', 'Raising prices for everyone', 'Paying taxes to the government'],
    'In the certain ERE, no one would hold cash. In a world of uncertainty, keeping money ready is a real service to its owner.', 'Why keep cash at all?'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'What does a general expectation that the PPM will soon fall do now?', 'Lowers the PPM now, as people spend', ['Raises the PPM now, as people save', 'Has no effect until the fall arrives', 'Forces banks to raise interest rates'],
    'People spend money sooner rather than hold units that will buy less, bringing the fall about at once.', 'Spend it before it loses value.'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'How does a clearing system economize on money?', 'By canceling debts against each other', ['By printing new money for traders who need it', 'By banning cash in large trades', 'By lending money at zero interest'],
    'If A owes B, B owes C, and so on to E who owes A ten ounces each, 50 ounces of debts clear without a single ounce of cash.', 'A circle of debts.'),
  q(4, 11, 'easy', 'Isn’t the demand for money unlimited, since everyone wants more?', 'No: effective demand is limited', ['Yes: no one has enough money', 'Yes: so prices must always rise', 'No: money is never demanded'],
    'Wanting is not demand. Demand is limited by the goods one can sell and by how much one wants to hold rather than spend.', 'Wish, or effective demand?'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard object to the word “hoarding”?', 'It falsely implies antisocial action', ['It is too old-fashioned a term', 'Hoarding never actually happens in real markets', 'It refers only to hoarding gold'],
    'An increase in the demand for money just means people get what they want: higher real cash balances. Nothing antisocial is involved.', 'It sounds vicious, but is it?'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'What Keynesian error does Rothbard see in arguing that wage cuts reduce demand and income?', 'It confuses rates with payrolls', ['Wages never fall in a recession at all', 'Keynes ignored the role of trade unions', 'Income doesn’t depend on spending at all'],
    'Lower wage rates lead to hiring the unemployed and more production, raising real income. A falling rate doesn’t mean a falling payroll.', 'Rate versus total.'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard object to mathematical “mutual determination” in the Keynesian system?', 'In human action, causes run one way', ['Mathematics is too hard for economists', 'Keynes used the wrong equations', 'Functions cannot be graphed'],
    'Reversible functions suit physics, where causes are unknown. In economics we know the causes: individual action.', 'X determines Y; does Y determine X?'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'How could banks earn income under 100-percent reserves, according to Rothbard?', 'By charging fees and lending saved funds', ['They could not: they would all close', 'By printing notes for their own use', 'By receiving subsidies from the state'],
    'Like any warehouse, they charge fees, and they can borrow saved money and relend it, earning the interest differential.', 'Warehouses charge fees.'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'How does Rothbard propose to enforce 100-percent reserves without arbitrary government fiat?', 'As part of the general law against fraud', ['By a constitutional amendment', 'By creating a new federal banking agency with wide powers', 'By letting banks vote on it'],
    'Revive the old rule, cited by Jevons, that a grant of goods not in existence is without operation. Banking can then be left free.', 'Fictitious receipts as fraud.'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'Why can money never be “neutral,” according to Rothbard?', 'Price changes never happen all at once', ['Governments always manipulate it', 'Gold is never perfectly divisible', 'Interest rates never stay the same from year to year'],
    'A change in the money relation shifts relative prices and incomes, creating gains for some and losses for others.', 'Which prices change first?'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'On a free market with gold money, why would there be no “exchange rate” between two places?', 'An ounce of gold is an ounce of gold', ['Transport costs make trade impossible', 'Each region would set its own rate', 'Governments would fix rates by treaty'],
    'There may be slight variations in the PPM within transport costs, but no deviation from par between the same money.', 'How do you exchange gold for gold?'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'What happened to silver as money in the late nineteenth century, in Rothbard’s account?', 'Western states imposed gold by coercion', ['The market freely abandoned silver', 'Silver drove gold out of circulation', 'Silver mines all ran out at once'],
    'He calls it a “coup d’état against silver.” Coexisting moneys should circulate at freely fluctuating exchange ratios.', 'A coup d’état.'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'Whose equation of exchange does Rothbard attack as a holistic fallacy?', 'Irving Fisher’s', ['Knut Wicksell’s', 'Alfred Marshall’s', 'David Ricardo’s'],
    'It treats money apart from individual action. Fisher also campaigned for a “stable” money.', 'An American who came from mathematical physics.'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'Why is the stabilization of the price level by the government an impossible program?', 'The PPM can’t even be defined or measured', ['The government lacks enough gold to do it', 'Prices never actually change in markets', 'Stable prices are always too high'],
    'Any index is arbitrary, and pumping money in or out distorts relative prices and production.', 'Measure what, exactly?'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'What did Irving Fisher say led him to seek a “stable yardstick of value”?', 'His background in mathematical physics', ['His losses in the stock market', 'His years working at a central bank in New York', 'His study of medieval coinage'],
    'Physics needs fixed units of measure; Fisher didn’t see the difference between physics and purposeful human action.', 'Where had he come from?'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'What, for Rothbard, is the key fact any business cycle theory must explain?', 'A sudden cluster of entrepreneurial errors', ['The regular rhythm of sunspots and harvests', 'Waves of public optimism and pessimism', 'The slow growth of the population'],
    'Hardheaded entrepreneurs don’t err en masse on a free market. The cause, explained in chapter 12, lies outside it.', 'Why do so many go bust at once?'),
  q(4, 11, 'hard', 'Rothbard mocks the Keynesian multiplier by computing a multiplier of 100,000 for what?', 'The income of the reader himself', ['The national defense budget', 'The output of the gold mines', 'The spending of the central bank'],
    'Since everyone else’s income is a “stable function” of social income, give the reader money and national income rises 100,000-fold.', 'Look in the mirror.'),
  q(4, 11, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard reject the term “function” in the study of human action?', 'It implies quantitative determinism', ['Functions are too hard to compute', 'Keynes defined the term poorly', 'Economics uses no numbers at all'],
    'People can change their actions at any time. “Function” suits the unmotivated, repeatable motion of inorganic matter.', 'Can a person change his mind?'),

  // Part V: Violent Intervention (chapter 12 and Power and Market, chapters 1–2). Chapter 12: The Economics of Violent Intervention in the Market
  q(5, 12, 'medium', 'Some economists, like Edwin Cannan, denied economics could analyze violent intervention. What is Rothbard’s view?', 'Violence is action, so it applies', ['Economics must stay silent on violence', 'Only historians may study intervention', 'Violence is irrational and beyond analysis'],
    'Economics is the praxeological analysis of human action, and violent interrelations are forms of action.', 'Is coercion a form of action?'),
  q(5, 12, 'easy', 'What is intervention, in Rothbard’s definition?', 'The intrusion of aggressive force into society', ['Any action taken by a government', 'Any regulation of prices by markets', 'Charity given to the poor by the rich, through the churches'],
    'It substitutes coercion for voluntary action. It doesn’t matter who wields the force, though empirically it is mostly States.', 'Coercion instead of what?'),
  q(5, 12, 'medium', 'Why is the force used by a defense agency against an invader not itself intervention?', 'It is counterintervention', ['Defense agencies are part of the State', 'Invaders consent to being stopped', 'Police force is always nonviolent'],
    'Inhibitory force defending persons and property maximizes the utility of noncriminals.', 'Who initiated the force?'),
  q(5, 12, 'medium', 'What simple query does Rothbard say economists never ask about “growth”?', 'What is so good about growth?', ['How should growth be measured?', 'Which country grows fastest?', 'Who will pay for the growth?'],
    'Growth smuggles in an unanalyzed ethical judgment. If it were the only value, why stop at 5 or 8 percent a year? Why not 50?', 'A question of values.'),
  q(5, 12, 'medium', 'What did Rothbard find curious about reported Soviet “growth”?', 'It hardly reached consumers', ['It was concentrated in consumers’ goods', 'It was faster than any Western country’s', 'It was financed by foreign investors'],
    'Growth was in steel and dams, not the standard of living, the be-all of production. Compulsory investment is really wasteful consumption by officials.', 'Iron and steel, but what about the consumer?'),
  q(5, 12, 'hard', 'How does Rothbard answer Galbraith’s claim in The Affluent Society that advertising creates consumers’ wants?', 'Each person must adopt his own values', ['Advertising in fact creates all wants', 'Wants are fixed by biology alone', 'Advertising is always honest and harmless'],
    'Advertising persuades; it can’t create wants. Galbraith is a determinist who exempts himself from the determination.', 'Who is immune to the ads, then?'),
  q(5, 12, 'hard', 'Which services, Rothbard notes, have historically been supplied by private enterprise, contrary to Galbraith?', 'Roads, mail, coinage, police and defense', ['Only food, clothing and shelter', 'None of the services governments provide', 'Only luxuries for the very rich'],
    'Every service assumed to be government’s alone, including courts and education, has been supplied privately. England had private roads and canals.', 'Even justice and defense.'),
  q(5, 12, 'medium', 'What are “pseudo warehouse receipts,” in Rothbard’s account of inflation?', 'Receipts beyond the specie held', ['Receipts for goods stored in warehouses', 'Bonds sold by the government to banks', 'Coins minted with too little metal in them'],
    'They look like genuine certificates but promise what could not be met if all holders asked at once: they are fraudulent.', 'More receipts than gold.'),
  q(5, 12, 'hard', 'How does inflation distort business accounting, according to Rothbard?', 'It shows false profits, so capital is consumed', ['It understates profits, deterring investment', 'It forces firms to keep two sets of books', 'It makes every firm’s books exactly right'],
    'Capital bought at low prices is replaced at high ones. Businessmen think they profit and consume capital, and old-equipment firms attract unjustified investment.', 'Replacement costs more than recorded.'),
  q(5, 12, 'easy', 'In the Austrian theory, what does bank credit expansion do to the interest rate?', 'Lowers it below the market rate, for a time', ['Raises it permanently above the market rate', 'Has no effect on interest rates at all', 'Fixes it at zero for as long as it lasts'],
    'Businessmen think more savings exist and malinvest. When factor owners spend their incomes, the time-preference rate reasserts itself: that is the cycle.', 'Cheap credit, briefly.'),
  q(5, 12, 'hard', 'Does an increase in the money supply lower the rate of interest, as mercantilists and Keynesians claimed?', 'Only temporarily, when it enters as credit', ['Yes, and permanently, given money demand', 'Never, under any circumstances whatever', 'Only if the government spends it first'],
    'In the long run, which isn’t very long, the market restores its time-preference rate. The reversion process is the business cycle.', 'Mercantilists and classicists were each half right.'),
  q(5, 12, 'medium', 'Why does every credit-expansion boom leave society poorer?', 'Resources are squandered', ['Prices fall too low after the boom', 'Workers are paid too much during it', 'Banks keep the extra money for themselves'],
    'Some projects are abandoned; others run but serve consumers less than without the boom.', 'Monuments of failure.'),
  q(5, 12, 'hard', 'How does Rothbard answer the claim that credit expansion is harmless when there are idle resources?', 'Idle capital is a hangover of malinvestment', ['Idle resources never exist on markets', 'Credit expansion always creates jobs', 'Idle workers should be drafted instead'],
    'Putting submarginal capital back to work redoubles distortions and delays recovery.', 'Why was it idle?'),
  q(5, 12, 'medium', 'What is the “ideal” condition for unlimited inflation, according to Rothbard?', 'A world fiat money from a world central bank', ['A gold standard managed by many nations', 'Free banking with no central bank', 'Many competing national paper currencies'],
    'With no external drain, the only limit left is the runaway boom that destroys the currency.', 'No other money to flee to.'),
  q(5, 12, 'hard', 'Why would recovery from a World State’s runaway inflation be far harder than Germany’s in 1923?', 'No earlier money would remain', ['Germany had a stronger army afterwards', 'World governments never repay their debts', 'Gold would be worth less than paper by then'],
    'By the regression theorem, a money can only arise from exchange with a previously existing money. Germany could lean on gold and foreign currencies.', 'The regression theorem.'),
  q(5, 12, 'medium', 'Who, in Rothbard’s conclusion, walks the economy along a “tightrope” between inflation and depression?', 'Government intervention', ['The free market, left to itself', 'Labor unions and big business', 'Foreign traders and speculators'],
    'The unhampered market would not suffer inflation, depression or unemployment; intervention creates the tightrope.', 'Who strung the tightrope?'),
  q(5, 12, 'hard', 'Rothbard says praxeology is Wertfrei. What does it leave to the citizen?', 'The ethical choice of political course', ['The calculation of correct and just prices', 'The measurement of everyone’s utility', 'The design of a better central bank'],
    'It shows that the free market leads to prosperity and order, coercion to conflict and poverty; then it retires from the scene.', 'Science, then values.'),
  q(5, 12, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard reject the argument that people “benefit” when the State forces them to pay for external benefits?', 'Their choices revealed otherwise', ['External benefits never exist at all', 'The State always measures benefits correctly', 'Benefits are only real if they are taxed'],
    'Preferences are known only through choices. If producing the benefit isn’t profitable, consumers value other uses of factors more.', 'What did they actually choose?'),

  // Power and Market, chapter 1: Defense Services on the Free Market
  q(5, 13, 'easy', 'What contradiction does Rothbard find in laissez-faire theorists’ defense of government?', 'The defender of property taxes', ['They want too little government spending', 'They oppose all forms of private property', 'They favor world government above all'],
    'A laissez-faire government must seize revenue by taxation and claim a compulsory monopoly of defense over a territory.', 'How is the defender financed?'),
  q(5, 13, 'medium', 'Laissez-faireists say defense is a “precondition” of the market, so the State must supply it. What is Rothbard’s reply?', 'Food and paper are preconditions too', ['Defense is not needed at all in a free market', 'Only the market can supply preconditions', 'The State should also supply all food'],
    'Thinking in large classes instead of marginal units proves far too much. Must the State supply everything indispensable?', 'What else is indispensable?'),
  q(5, 13, 'medium', 'Rothbard notes an Argentinian lives in “anarchy” toward a citizen of Uruguay. What does that show?', 'Peace needs no single government', ['Argentina should conquer Uruguay', 'All nations need a world government', 'Trade between nations is impossible'],
    'Citizens of different countries trade and settle disputes without a common ruler. Why not northern and southern Montana?', 'No common ruler, yet no chaos.'),
  q(5, 13, 'hard', 'Where does the logic of secession lead, for Rothbard, once separate states are admitted?', 'Down to each individual seceding', ['To one world government above all', 'To the union of all neighboring states', 'Only to the secession of large regions'],
    'If Canada and the U.S. may be separate, why not the South, New York City, Manhattan, each block, each house, each person?', 'Where does one logically stop?'),
  q(5, 13, 'medium', 'Which bodies of law, Rothbard says, were largely developed by privately competitive judges?', 'The law merchant and common law', ['The Napoleonic Code and ancient Roman law', 'Constitutional law and tax law', 'Labor law and antitrust law'],
    'Litigants sought out judges for expertise. The fairs of Champagne had freely competitive courts.', 'Medieval traders chose their courts.'),
  q(5, 13, 'medium', 'Who, Rothbard suggests, would likely supply police and judicial services on a free market?', 'Insurance companies, by subscription', ['Churches, through voluntary tithes', 'Labor unions, for their own members and families', 'Local landlords, for their tenants'],
    'It would be to insurers’ direct advantage to reduce crime as much as possible.', 'Who pays when crimes occur?'),
  q(5, 13, 'hard', 'What “cutoff point” for appeals might a libertarian legal code adopt, according to Rothbard?', 'Any two courts’ decision binds', ['The ruling of one Supreme Court', 'A unanimous vote of all the judges in the country', 'A referendum of all subscribers'],
    'Every legal system needs a point where judging stops and punishment begins. Two contesting parties suggest two courts.', 'There are two parties to every case.'),
  q(5, 13, 'hard', 'Couldn’t a private defense agency turn criminal? How does Rothbard answer?', 'Competing agencies could put it down', ['Agencies would never turn criminal', 'The courts would abolish it at once', 'Its customers would all forgive it'],
    'No legalized channel for aggression would exist, and competing agencies are real checks and balances, unlike branches of one government.', 'What would a criminal agency lack?'),
  q(5, 13, 'medium', 'What is the worst that could happen in an experiment with a stateless society, in Rothbard’s view?', 'The State would be re-established', ['Society would collapse into chaos', 'Everyone would become a criminal', 'The economy would cease to exist'],
    'And since the State is what we have now, the experiment has nothing to lose and everything to gain.', 'What do we have now?'),
  q(5, 13, 'hard', 'Which British political philosopher does Rothbard quote on the contradiction of laissez faire?', 'Auberon Herbert', ['Herbert Spencer', 'John Stuart Mill', 'Edmund Burke'],
    'Herbert showed that if A compels B, or B compels A, co-operation is not secured.', 'A voluntaryist, not Spencer himself.'),

  // Power and Market, chapter 2: Fundamentals of Intervention
  q(5, 14, 'easy', 'What are Rothbard’s three types of intervention?', 'Autistic, binary and triangular', ['Monetary, fiscal and regulatory', 'Local, national and global', 'Direct, indirect and hidden'],
    'Autistic coerces one subject alone; binary forces an exchange with the intervener; triangular controls exchanges between two others.', 'One, two, or three parties.'),
  q(5, 14, 'medium', 'Which of these is a binary intervention?', 'Taxation by the State', ['Price control', 'Prohibiting a religion', 'A minimum wage law'],
    'Highway robbery, taxes, conscription and compulsory jury service force a “gift” or exchange with the intervener.', 'A forced transfer to the intervener.'),
  q(5, 14, 'medium', 'Which intervention category, Rothbard says, did economists curiously recognize as the only kind?', 'Triangular intervention', ['Autistic intervention', 'Binary intervention', 'Monetary and fiscal intervention'],
    'Preoccupied with market exchanges, they overlooked actions outside the money nexus, like taxation and conscription.', 'Price and product controls.'),
  q(5, 14, 'hard', 'Whose analysis of net taxpayers versus net tax-consumers does Rothbard praise?', 'John C. Calhoun’s', ['Karl Marx’s', 'Adam Smith’s', 'Thomas Jefferson’s'],
    'In his Disquisition on Government, Calhoun saw that taxes are bounties to those who receive more than they pay: the ruling class.', 'A South Carolina statesman.'),
  q(5, 14, 'medium', 'Does majority support make the State voluntary, according to Rothbard?', 'No: majority coercion is still coercion', ['Yes: democracy makes it voluntary', 'Yes: if more than two-thirds approve', 'No: because majorities never support States'],
    'All States rest on at least passive majority support; as Hume said, government is founded on opinion. But the majority is not everyone.', 'Is the majority everyone?'),
  q(5, 14, 'hard', 'Mises points out a paradox in “progressives” who call consumers too ignorant to buy wisely. What is it?', 'They trust the same people to vote wisely', ['They themselves never buy anything', 'They want consumers to spend even more money', 'They oppose all forms of advertising'],
    'Consumers have direct tests of results; voters have none, and must follow long chains of reasoning to judge policies.', 'Shoppers, or voters?'),
  q(5, 14, 'medium', 'Why can’t voters easily find out a ruler’s errors, compared with consumers?', 'Politics has no direct test of success', ['Votes are counted in secret', 'Politicians never make mistakes', 'Voters are less intelligent than shoppers'],
    'The government can inflate and blame speculators. Schumpeter: the prettiest girl can’t keep a bad cigarette selling, but politics has no such safeguard.', 'Profits and losses, or nothing?'),
  q(5, 14, 'hard', 'Rothbard says critics of commercial advertising aim at the wrong target. Where would their charges be relevant?', 'Political campaign advertising', ['Advertising of patent medicines', 'Advertising aimed at children', 'Billboards along public highways'],
    'Where there is no direct test of results, the ability to “sell” propaganda wins.', 'Where can’t you return the product?'),

  // Part VI: Triangular and Binary Intervention (Power and Market, chapters 3–4). Chapter 3: Triangular Intervention
  q(6, 15, 'easy', 'What does an effective maximum price control create?', 'An artificial shortage', ['An unsold surplus of goods', 'Lower costs for producers', 'Greater output of the good'],
    'Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied. Queues, favoritism and black markets follow.', 'More buyers than goods at that price.'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'Governments impose selective price controls “as long as the product is in short supply.” What does Rothbard say?', 'The control itself causes the shortage', ['The control ends the shortage quickly', 'Shortages vanish once prices are fixed', 'Only rationing can create a shortage'],
    'Resources shift to substitutes, so the shortage grows worse as long as the control lasts.', 'Which is the cause, which the effect?'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'Under bimetallism, the state fixes silver at 16 to 1 against gold, but the market moves to 15 to 1. What happens?', 'Silver, undervalued, disappears from circulation', ['Gold, overvalued, disappears from circulation abroad', 'Both metals stay in circulation as before', 'Prices of all goods fall by a sixteenth'],
    'Fixing the ratio puts a maximum price on one money and a minimum on the other: Gresham’s law in action.', 'Which money is “too cheap”?'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'What do usury laws, by capping interest rates, create?', 'An artificial shortage of credit', ['A flood of cheap loans for the poor and for small business', 'Higher saving and more investment', 'A surplus of funds in the banks'],
    'Savings fall and marginal borrowers are shut out. Illegal loans carry higher rates to cover the risk of arrest.', 'A price ceiling on loans.'),
  q(6, 15, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard call ration tickets a kind of “double money”?', 'Consumers need tickets as well as money to buy', ['They are printed by the central bank', 'They can be exchanged for gold coin at any bank in the land', 'They double the money supply at once'],
    'Rationing cripples money’s function of letting consumers direct production, and non-transferable coupons distort spending further.', 'Money alone is no longer enough.'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'When the government grants a monopoly privilege, what factor is being monopolized, in the long run?', 'The right to enter the industry', ['The capital of the privileged firm', 'The labor of the firm’s workers', 'The product sold to consumers'],
    'On the free market that right is open to all. Monopoly returns are imputed to the privilege of entry.', 'What is everyone else denied?'),
  q(6, 15, 'hard', 'Why do occupational licenses for workers always raise their wages, while licenses to businesses may not raise prices?', 'Firms can expand; labor can’t', ['Workers are always better organized', 'Businesses never ask for licenses', 'Licenses to firms are always cheaper'],
    'Licensed grocers can grow to fill the gap; excluding workers from an occupation always reduces its labor supply.', 'Can a worker double himself?'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'What is Rothbard’s answer to government quality standards meant to protect consumers?', 'Consumers judge quality; courts punish fraud', ['Standards should be set by the producers’ own associations', 'Consumers need no protection at all', 'Only experts can judge what is safe'],
    'Selling straw as breakfast food is fraud, a breach of contract punishable at law. Standards just freeze technology and protect incumbents.', 'Who decides what “quality” means?'),
  q(6, 15, 'easy', 'Rothbard imagines Jones and Smith putting a 1,000-percent tariff on everything from outside their farm. What happens?', 'They work dawn to dusk for bare subsistence', ['They become rich by keeping all their money at home', 'They end unemployment and live in luxury', 'They export more than they import'],
    'The tariff principle leads logically to self-sufficiency: poverty for all and death for most of the world’s population.', 'Self-sufficiency at what level?'),
  q(6, 15, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard reject the “infant industry” argument for protection?', 'If it would truly pay, private investors would enter', ['New industries never succeed anywhere', 'Infant industries always fail without permanent protection', 'Tariffs always raise industry output'],
    'Subsidizing new plants deprives consumers of the advantages of capital sunk in old locations. Mises: worthwhile new plants can compete unaided.', 'Why are entrepreneurs staying away?'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'What happens when high-wage countries bar immigrants, according to Rothbard?', 'Capital flows abroad to low-wage areas instead', ['Wages equalize everywhere much faster than before', 'Foreign workers become richer', 'World production rises sharply'],
    'Equalization continues through capital export, but resources end up misplaced: too much capital and labor abroad.', 'If workers can’t move, what can?'),
  q(6, 15, 'hard', 'How does conscription work as a monopoly privilege, in Rothbard’s analysis?', 'It raises wages of those left', ['It lowers wages in every industry', 'It creates new private jobs for returning soldiers', 'It has no effect on the labor market'],
    'The government commandeers labor below market rates, while the remaining workers face less competition.', 'Fewer competitors in the labor market.'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'What do government unemployment benefits do, in Rothbard’s account?', 'Subsidize union-made unemployment', ['Cure unemployment by raising total spending in the economy', 'Encourage workers to find new jobs', 'Lower wages throughout the economy'],
    'They remove potential competitors from the labor market, letting union wage rates be maintained, at taxpayers’ expense.', 'Who benefits from keeping competitors idle?'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'Chain store taxes and bans on pushcart peddlers have what effect?', 'They penalize efficient forms, privileging the inefficient', ['They protect consumers from bad and dangerous products', 'They have no effect on competition', 'They help the most efficient businesses'],
    'Penalties on market forms injure consumers for the benefit of less efficient but more politically influential competitors.', 'Who is penalized, who is protected?'),
  q(6, 15, 'easy', 'What is the only viable definition of monopoly, according to Rothbard in Power and Market?', 'A grant of privilege from the government', ['A single seller in any market', 'A firm with a large market share', 'A company that earns high profits year after year'],
    'So the antitrust laws can’t reduce monopoly; only removing the government’s own grants can.', 'Where does it come from?'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'What do the antitrust laws actually accomplish, in Rothbard’s view?', 'Harassment of efficient firms', ['A real reduction in monopoly power', 'Lower prices for most consumers', 'More competition in every industry'],
    'Vague terms let officials decide after the fact what is a crime. Only the government can restrain trade, and antitrust is one way it does.', 'Who decides what is “monopolizing”?'),
  q(6, 15, 'hard', 'If cartels must be broken up because of “collusion,” what must antitrusters logically outlaw, says Rothbard?', 'All corporations and partnerships', ['Only foreign cartels and trusts that sell here', 'Only very large manufacturers', 'Only firms in the oil business'],
    'There is no conceptual difference between a cartel, a merger and a corporation: all pool assets voluntarily.', 'What else pools assets?'),
  q(6, 15, 'hard', 'What question does Rothbard ask of conservation laws that favor future generations?', 'What did the future ever do for us?', ['How can resources be used up faster and more cheaply?', 'Why should anyone save at all?', 'Who will own the forests next year?'],
    'They force a lower ratio of consumption to saving than people prefer. The future, likely richer, becomes a present that is restricted in turn.', 'Pie in the sky.'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'On the free market, what determines how much of a natural resource is “conserved”?', 'Time preferences, via the interest rate', ['Government quotas on extraction', 'The physical size of the deposit', 'The number of firms in the industry'],
    'Owners maximize present value. Lower time preference means more investment for the future and more conservation.', 'Present versus future.'),
  q(6, 15, 'hard', 'Advocates of eminent domain say “society” may use any land for “its” purposes. What do they unknowingly concede?', 'A Georgist claim to land', ['That private property is absolute', 'That land has no economic value', 'That roads should be built privately'],
    'Since “society” doesn’t exist as an entity, ownership devolves on the government or those it privileges.', 'Everyone’s aliquot share of land.'),
  q(6, 15, 'hard', 'How does Rothbard analyze the bribery of government officials?', 'As buying a license to do business', ['As a crime with no economic effect', 'As a kind of tax paid to the state', 'As a gift from the rich to the poor'],
    'The service bought is non-enforcement of an edict. Praxeologically it’s identical to buying a government license.', 'What is the official selling?'),
  q(6, 15, 'medium', 'What does Rothbard say about governments’ claim that minting must be a state monopoly?', 'Private minters would suffice', ['Only governments can test coins honestly', 'Private coins would always be fake', 'Coins are not needed in modern economies'],
    'Reputation and prosecution for fraud protect machine-tool buyers; they’d protect coin users too. Government has a long record of debasement.', 'Who has the record of debasing coins?'),
  q(6, 15, 'hard', 'What is Rothbard’s “Lebensraum” argument about immigration barriers?', 'They cause “overpopulation” and invite war', ['They prevent wars by keeping peoples apart', 'They are needed to protect native cultures', 'They have nothing to do with war'],
    'A government’s power to keep people out assumes it owns all its territory. Barriers, like tariffs, can be a prelude to war.', 'Who has the right to keep people out?'),

  // Chapter 4: Binary Intervention: Taxation
  q(6, 16, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard say all government spending on resources is a form of consumption?', 'Officials decree what is produced', ['Governments never invest in anything', 'Officials eat the goods they buy', 'Taxes are spent only on food'],
    'Government officials are, in effect, the “consumers” whose wishes alter production. Talk of government “investment” is fallacious.', 'Whose wishes decide?'),
  q(6, 16, 'easy', 'What is Rothbard’s “rather radical” first law of tax incidence?', 'No tax can be shifted forward', ['Every tax is passed on to consumers', 'All taxes fall on the rich', 'Only income taxes can be shifted'],
    'Prices are set by stock and demand, not by costs. If firms could charge more, they would have done so before the tax.', 'From seller to buyer?'),
  q(6, 16, 'medium', 'A $1.00 movie ticket shows “price 85 cents, tax 15 cents.” What is the real price, per Rothbard?', '$1.00', ['85 cents', '15 cents', '$1.15'],
    'The 85 cents is the firm’s income after tax, which may well have been reduced to pay it.', 'What does the moviegoer hand over?'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'How does a partial excise tax raise the price of the taxed good, if not by being shifted forward?', 'Marginal firms leave, so less is produced', ['Firms add the tax to their costs', 'Consumers demand more of the good after the tax', 'The government sets a higher price'],
    'Nonspecific factors exit the taxed industry, and the smaller stock commands a higher price. The burden falls mostly on specific factors.', 'Less of it gets produced.'),
  q(6, 16, 'medium', 'Why does an income tax raise the proportion of income consumed?', 'Lower assets raise time preference', ['People want to spend before taxes rise any further', 'Savings are taxed but spending is not', 'Banks pay less interest after taxes'],
    'Given the time-preference schedule, a man with fewer real monetary assets prefers present goods more. Taxes fall more heavily on saving.', 'Poorer people discount the future more.'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'What does the double taxation of corporate income encourage, in Rothbard’s view?', 'Earnings frozen in the original firm', ['More dividends paid out to all the stockholders', 'Faster growth of new companies', 'Lower prices for consumers'],
    'Stockholders leave more undistributed than on a free market, discouraging mobile investment in other firms.', 'Why pay out dividends that get taxed twice?'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'A 20 percent consumption tax, Rothbard shows, becomes equivalent to what?', 'A 15 percent income tax', ['A 20 percent sales tax', 'A 25 percent income tax', 'No tax at all on income'],
    'The individual reduces both consumption and saving, so every consumption tax is shifted into an income tax at a lower rate.', 'Irving Fisher’s plan fails.'),
  q(6, 16, 'medium', 'What happens to a property’s capital value when a permanent property tax is levied?', 'It falls', ['It rises', 'It stays the same', 'It falls to zero'],
    'At 5 percent interest, a 1 percent tax drops a $10,000 property to $8,333. The burden falls on the owner at the time.', 'Capitalization.'),
  q(6, 16, 'easy', 'What does a progressive income tax penalize, according to Rothbard?', 'Service to consumers, or market merit', ['Only the idle rich who never work', 'Foreign investors and speculators', 'Consumption more than saving'],
    'Incomes reflect service to consumers. Penalizing the best servers hurts them and consumers alike, and cripples incentives to work and save.', 'Who earns more on a free market?'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard call the conservative argument that progressive taxes cut savings a weak one?', 'Saving has no special merit', ['Progressive taxes in fact raise saving', 'The rich never save anything at all', 'Savings have no effect on growth'],
    'Any coerced deviation from market time preferences is a loss. By that logic, conservatives should tax the poor to subsidize the rich.', 'Is saving better than spending?'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'What would Henry George’s 100 percent tax on ground rent actually yield, according to Rothbard?', 'No revenue at all', ['All the revenue government needs', 'Double the present tax revenue', 'Revenue only from city land'],
    'Taxing away all rent makes sites effectively free; the best sites are grabbed by first comers and wasted, as under price control.', 'What happens when rent goes to zero?'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'What theoretical flaw does Rothbard find in taxing ground rent alone?', 'Land rent can’t be isolated', ['Land is too easy to hide from assessors', 'Ground rent does not exist at all', 'Landowners would simply move away'],
    'There is no way to divide a property’s gross rent between ground land, interest and wages, or to assess unsold property.', 'Which part is the land?'),
  q(6, 16, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard urge economists to drop the search for the “just tax”?', 'Taxation must first be shown just', ['All taxes are equally just in the eyes of the law', 'Only flat taxes can be just', 'The free market sets just taxes'],
    'Economists abandoned the “just price” for the market price, but there is no market tax, and no neutral tax.', 'Just compared with what?'),
  q(6, 16, 'medium', 'What does Rothbard say about the canon that taxes “should” be hard to evade?', 'Evading an unjust tax may help', ['Evasion is always the worst crime', 'Taxes should be impossible to evade', 'Evasion has no economic effects'],
    'None of Adam Smith’s supposedly self-evident canons is a canon at all; economics cannot decide among ethical views.', 'What if the tax is unjust?'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard say advocates of the free market err in condemning tax “loopholes” and exemptions?', 'An exemption extends liberty', ['Loopholes help only the very rich', 'Exemptions always raise other taxes', 'Uniform taxes are the free market’s rule'],
    'Uniformity is a canon only if the tax is just. The free society means equality of liberty, not “equality before the law.”', 'Is escaping an unjust burden a privilege?'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'How does Rothbard answer Seligman’s “ability to pay” claim that money makes money with increasing returns?', 'Fortunes must keep serving consumers', ['Money does in fact multiply itself', 'The rich always grow richer and richer forever', 'Seligman’s claim is proven by data'],
    'If money made money, a few would own all the world’s wealth. Income comes from current service, and unwise owners dissipate fortunes.', 'Then why don’t a few own everything?'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'What does the equal-sacrifice theory of taxation actually imply, even on its own grounds?', 'Possibly regressive taxation', ['Always steeply progressive taxation', 'A flat tax on every income', 'No taxes on anyone at all'],
    'Depending on how fast the utility of money declines, it may call for the rich to pay more in amount but less in proportion.', 'It depends on utility curves.'),
  q(6, 16, 'medium', 'If taxes were levied by the benefit principle, what would government shrink to?', 'Defense alone, paid by the weak', ['A welfare state for the needy', 'A system of public monopolies', 'A government with no services'],
    'Welfare and privileges would be pointless if recipients paid for what they got. The infirm benefit most from police.', 'Who benefits most from police?'),
  q(6, 16, 'medium', 'What flaw does Rothbard find in financing government by voluntary gifts?', 'Service flows regardless of payment', ['People would give too much money', 'Gifts to governments are illegal in most countries', 'Charities would stop operating'],
    'Disjoining payment from service leads to excess demand and too few funds. A set price for defense would be more efficient.', 'Free riders.'),
  q(6, 16, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard say a poll tax, or voting tax, is “not really a tax”?', 'It is a price for joining', ['It is too small to matter', 'It is paid only by the rich', 'It is refunded to everyone after voting'],
    'It charges for participating in the State organization. Even so, government employees remain tax consumers, not taxpayers.', 'A membership fee.'),

  // Part VII: Government, Ethics and Policy (Power and Market, chapters 5–7). Chapter 5: Binary Intervention: Government Expenditures
  q(7, 17, 'medium', 'What does government subsidy open the way to, according to Rothbard?', 'The political means to wealth', ['A fairer distribution of wealth for everyone', 'Faster growth for every efficient industry', 'An end to all poverty within a generation'],
    'Earnings are severed from service to consumers and depend on gaining control of the State apparatus.', 'Not production and exchange.'),
  q(7, 17, 'easy', 'What do all subsidies do, in Rothbard’s analysis?', 'Penalize the efficient to benefit the inefficient', ['Reward the efficient for serving consumers', 'Have no effect on the allocation of resources in the economy', 'Help consumers at no cost to anyone at all'],
    'They prolong the life of inefficient firms and keep factors out of their most value-productive uses.', 'Who pays, and who is kept afloat?'),
  q(7, 17, 'medium', 'How does Rothbard view government drives against “charity rackets” and street beggars?', 'They channel giving into state-approved routes', ['They protect the poor from greedy donors', 'They increase the total amount of private charity given', 'They help beggars find better work quickly'],
    'Mayor LaGuardia drove organ-grinders off the streets despite a Park Avenue woman’s plea for her favorite one.', 'Who was the organ-grinder’s real friend?'),
  q(7, 17, 'medium', 'Why can’t a government that gives a service away free allocate it rationally?', 'With no price, all uses are kept on one plane', ['Free services are always of poor quality', 'Government workers refuse to do the work', 'Taxpayers vote against every free service the state offers'],
    'Without pricing, submarginal uses aren’t excluded. Should a road go in place A or B? Only an official’s whim can decide.', 'What excludes the least important uses?'),
  q(7, 17, 'hard', 'Conservatives call for putting the post office on a “business basis.” What does Rothbard say that really means?', 'Charging a monopoly price to cover inefficiency', ['Selling it off at once to the highest private bidder', 'Running it exactly like a private firm', 'Ending all deliveries to rural areas'],
    'A compulsory monopoly can’t operate on business principles. Higher fares may even raise deficits, as with New York’s subway.', 'Raising prices on captive users.'),
  q(7, 17, 'medium', 'For economics, what is the essence of socialism, whatever its form?', 'Total coercive State dictation over the economy', ['The equal distribution of income and wealth to all', 'Government by the working class alone', 'Public ownership chosen by free elections'],
    'It may be monarchical or proletarian, may equalize or increase inequality. Rightist or leftist, any increase in State control moves toward it.', 'What do all its forms share?'),
  q(7, 17, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard say government lending is an underestimated form of collectivism?', 'A lender is an entrepreneur and part owner', ['Government loans are never repaid at all, by anyone', 'Loans are kept secret from the public', 'Only banks may lend to private firms'],
    'Loans and loan guarantees to private enterprise create many centers of government ownership, whatever their legal status.', 'What does lending make you?'),
  q(7, 17, 'easy', 'Who takes the “long view” of resources: private owners or government officials?', 'Private owners, who own capital value', ['Government officials, who serve the public', 'Both equally, since time preference is the same', 'Neither, since resources can’t be conserved'],
    'Officials own only the current use, not the capital value, and are transitory, so they milk resources as quickly as they can.', 'Who can sell the asset?'),
  q(7, 17, 'hard', 'In Rothbard’s view, the right of ownership includes what right, which shows “public” owners don’t really own?', 'The right to sell, or disown', ['The right to vote on its use', 'The right to tax its income', 'The right to inherit it'],
    '“If I cannot sell a thing, it is evident that I do not really own it.” The rulers are the true owners of “public” property.', 'Can a citizen sell his share of the post office?'),
  q(7, 17, 'medium', 'Why couldn’t a political opposition long survive under socialism, according to Rothbard?', 'The ruling party controls every livelihood', ['Elections would be held too infrequently', 'Opposition parties would have no ideas', 'Citizens would all agree with the rulers once they saw the results'],
    'The party owning all means of production decides how much newsprint and money the opposition gets, and each opponent’s livelihood.', 'Who allocates the newsprint?'),
  q(7, 17, 'hard', 'What contradiction does Rothbard find in the idea of an “independent judiciary” in a democracy?', 'If truly independent, it is an oligarchy', ['Judges are always elected by the people', 'Courts can never overrule a legislature', 'Independence makes judges more democratic'],
    'If elected or reappointed, judges aren’t independent; if independent, they rule within their sphere as an oligarchic dictatorship.', 'Independent of whom?'),

  // Chapter 6: Antimarket Ethics: A Praxeological Critique
  q(7, 18, 'medium', 'People are said to be too ignorant of their interests, so government experts must decide. What contradiction does Rothbard point out?', 'The same people are trusted to vote for the experts', ['Experts are always more ignorant than the voters who elect them', 'Governments never hire real experts at all', 'Ignorant people never buy anything anyway'],
    'People have a direct test of their interests on the market, but can’t follow the long reasoning needed to choose rulers or policies.', 'Incompetent as buyers, competent as voters?'),
  q(7, 18, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard say forcing a man to do the “moral” thing makes no moral sense?', 'Without freedom to choose, there is no morality', ['Moral rules are always mere opinion anyway', 'Forced virtue lasts longer than virtue freely chosen by the individual', 'Only the State can define what is moral'],
    'Is a man confined to prison moral because he doesn’t spend his time in saloons? Coercion deprives him of the chance to be moral.', 'The prisoner and the saloon.'),
  q(7, 18, 'hard', 'Why does Rothbard argue that equality of income is a conceptually impossible goal?', 'Each person is in a different place', ['The rich would always hide their money', 'Equal incomes would be too expensive', 'People would refuse to accept equal pay'],
    'How can a New Yorker’s enjoyment of the Manhattan skyline be equalized with an Indian’s swim in the Ganges? Real incomes can’t be equalized.', 'The skyline and the Ganges.'),
  q(7, 18, 'medium', 'What about “equality of opportunity,” offered as a substitute for equality of income?', 'It is just as meaningless', ['It is the free market’s true aim', 'It is achieved by public schooling', 'It is the only goal worth pursuing'],
    'Diversity of location rules it out: the New Yorker and the Indian can’t have equal opportunity to sail around Manhattan.', 'Same problem, different word.'),
  q(7, 18, 'medium', 'How does Rothbard see freedom and security against aggression?', 'As two sides of the same coin', ['As opposites that must be traded off', 'As goals only the State can provide', 'As luxuries for advanced economies'],
    'The State constantly invades liberty and security. Those who want masters may submit voluntarily, but why force others to join?', 'Can you have one without the other?'),
  q(7, 18, 'medium', 'How does Rothbard answer nostalgia for the secure, “unalienated” status society of the Middle Ages?', 'It was neither secure nor free; skill was wasted', ['It truly was a golden age for craftsmen', 'Medieval peasants were the happiest people who ever lived', 'Alienation began only with the computer'],
    'The carpenter’s son had to be a carpenter. The division of labor vastly expands the work a man can choose.', 'Was the carpenter’s son free to choose?'),
  q(7, 18, 'hard', 'What, for Rothbard, has been the greatest “charity” in the long run?', 'Selfish capital investment and innovation', ['Government welfare payments to the poor and the elderly', 'Church tithes collected over centuries', 'Gifts from wealthy philanthropists'],
    'Poverty has been tamed by our ancestors’ enterprise, mostly for “selfish” motives. Coerced charity drains production and creates a caste of poor.', 'Adam Smith’s truth again.'),
  q(7, 18, 'hard', 'Rothbard says a consistent altruist should actually praise the pursuit of money. Why?', 'Money income reflects service to others', ['Money buys charity for the poor', 'Rich people give away more money than anyone else', 'Altruists secretly love money'],
    'Psychic income and leisure are purely personal gains; a consistent altruist would have to deny workers leisure.', 'Whom does earning money serve?'),
  q(7, 18, 'easy', 'Does the free market deal only in “material” goods, as the charge of materialism implies?', 'No: it deals in exchangeable goods', ['Yes: it ignores all spiritual values', 'Yes: services are never traded', 'No: it deals only in money'],
    'Many nonmaterial goods, like concerts and lectures, are exchanged on the market.', 'Can you buy a concert ticket?'),
  q(7, 18, 'medium', 'Critics say the free market is the “law of the jungle.” What does Rothbard reply?', 'Statism brings back the jungle; the market tames it', ['The market is a jungle, but a productive and useful one', 'The jungle is natural and good for mankind', 'Only the strong survive under any system'],
    'The contractual society wrests order from chaos and lets the “weak” live royally compared to the “strong” in the jungle.', 'Who is really waging the war of all against all?'),
  q(7, 18, 'hard', 'If refusing to exchange counts as “economic power” or coercion, what follows, according to Rothbard?', 'Violence must be used to force exchanges', ['Nothing, since it is only a metaphor', 'All exchanges become perfectly voluntary', 'The economy becomes far more efficient'],
    'There is no middle ground: either only physical violence is illegal coercion, or refusal to exchange is, and must be fought with violence.', 'There is no middle ground.'),
  q(7, 18, 'medium', 'What does libertarian doctrine aim to maximize and eradicate, according to Rothbard?', 'Power over nature; power of man over man', ['State power; individual power over others', 'Equality of income; inequality of wealth', 'Wealth of nations; poverty of individuals'],
    'Albert Jay Nock made a similar distinction between social power and State power.', 'Nature, or other men?'),
  q(7, 18, 'medium', 'Why does Rothbard prefer “backward” to “underdeveloped” for poor countries?', 'It pins the blame on their own policies', ['It is more polite and diplomatic', 'It implies foreigners must develop them', 'It describes their culture more precisely'],
    '“Underdeveloped” implies some external force failed to develop them. Poor countries progress by letting enterprise and investment work.', 'Who is responsible?'),
  q(7, 18, 'hard', 'What does Rothbard argue about “human rights” versus property rights?', 'There are no rights but property rights', ['Human rights matter more than property', 'Property rights belong to corporations', 'The two kinds of rights always conflict'],
    'Each man owns himself; “human rights” are really property rights in one’s person and in what one produces or acquires.', 'Self-ownership.'),
  q(7, 18, 'hard', 'How does Rothbard resolve Justice Holmes’s case of falsely crying “fire” in a crowded theater?', 'As a violation of property rights', ['As proof that speech must be limited by public policy', 'As a matter for the legislature', 'As a harmless joke under free speech'],
    'An owner who does it defrauds his patrons; a patron who does it violates the owner’s property and others’ contracts.', 'Who owns the theater?'),
  q(7, 18, 'hard', 'Whose “Law of Equal Freedom” does Rothbard defend against Professor Oliver’s criticism?', 'Herbert Spencer’s', ['John Locke’s', 'Thomas Paine’s', 'Benjamin Tucker’s'],
    'Rothbard says its proviso clause is unnecessary and that Oliver attacks straw-man definitions of “infringement.”', 'An English Victorian philosopher.'),

  // Chapter 7: Conclusion: Economics and Public Policy
  q(7, 19, 'medium', 'What is the economist’s role in a purely free society, according to Rothbard?', 'Purely educational', ['To forecast prices for businesses', 'To plan the national economy', 'To set interest rates'],
    'Entrepreneurs, not economists, forecast consumer demand. The economist’s usefulness expands when government intervenes.', 'Not a business technologist.'),
  q(7, 19, 'medium', 'Why does the economist come “into his own” when government intervenes?', 'Its consequences need long chains of reasoning', ['Governments pay economists the most', 'Only trained economists can run government firms efficiently', 'Intervention makes forecasting easier'],
    'A businessman knows the effects of more demand for butter; judging an income tax requires long praxeological analysis.', 'Butter versus an income tax.'),
  q(7, 19, 'hard', 'Why can’t an economist advise a client without leaving value-free science?', 'Advising commits him to the client’s ends', ['Economists are always politically biased toward their employers', 'Clients never tell the truth about goals', 'Economics has no technical content'],
    'An economist advising the government on manipulating the money market commits himself to the desirability of that manipulation.', 'Whose ends is he serving?'),
  q(7, 19, 'hard', 'What “shortcut” to policy conclusions does Rothbard reject in utilitarian economists?', 'Assuming everyone’s ends are the same', ['Assuming consumers always know best', 'Assuming markets never make any mistakes at all', 'Assuming prices measure all values'],
    'Advocates of price control may favor it anyway, from love of power or patronage, even knowing it causes shortages.', 'Do the OPA director’s goals match yours?'),
  q(7, 19, 'easy', 'What two principles sum up Rothbard’s comparison of politico-economic systems?', 'The market principle and the hegemonic principle', ['The capitalist and the socialist principles', 'The democratic and the monarchical principles of rule', 'The national and the international principles'],
    'Freedom, mutual benefit, peace and calculation against coercion, exploitation, war and calculational chaos.', 'Voluntary or coerced?'),
  q(7, 19, 'medium', 'How does Rothbard handle “mixed” political systems in his two-principle scheme?', 'Break them into blends of the two principles', ['Treat them as a third, separate principle of their own', 'Ignore them as too complex to analyze', 'Classify them by their official ideology'],
    'On Crusoe’s and Friday’s island, there are only voluntary and coerced relations; every system mixes them.', 'Components of a mixture.'),
  q(7, 19, 'medium', 'Is the purely free economy the only stable system, according to Rothbard?', 'Praxeologically yes; psychologically in doubt', ['Yes, in every sense, beyond doubt', 'No: the mixed economy is most stable', 'No: socialism is the most stable system ever devised by man'],
    'It creates no economic problems of its own, but those who seek power or plunder may push society back down the hegemonic road.', 'Economics versus human nature.'),
  q(7, 19, 'hard', 'In how many ways does Rothbard say consumers paid for the American farm support program?', 'Four', ['One', 'Two', 'Seven'],
    'In taxes, higher prices, wasted surpluses, and forgone products in unsupported lines. Production quotas followed: interventions breed interventions.', 'Taxes, prices, surpluses and…'),
];

